1
00:00:00,170 --> 00:00:05,120
Well, I hope you had a bit of fun playing around with the sprites and the code blocks, because in

2
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:09,200
this lesson we are going to be exploring some of the code blocks together.

3
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:13,670
So let's begin with an important what is code?

4
00:00:13,970 --> 00:00:18,410
Code is basically just a set of instructions for a computer.

5
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:20,900
You see, computers actually aren't very clever.

6
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:25,850
Well, they aren't very clever at figuring out what you mean, like humans are.

7
00:00:26,150 --> 00:00:32,900
They need very clear and specific instructions, step by step, in order to do what we want them to

8
00:00:32,900 --> 00:00:33,470
do.

9
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:38,450
Imagine you're telling someone how to make a sandwich for a human.

10
00:00:38,450 --> 00:00:43,130
You might just say, please make me a peanut butter and jelly sandwich.

11
00:00:43,670 --> 00:00:46,760
But for a computer, you would have to be much more specific.

12
00:00:46,760 --> 00:00:50,540
Like take one slice of bread and place it on a plate.

13
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:53,060
Take the jar of peanut butter and open it.

14
00:00:53,060 --> 00:00:58,460
Insert a knife and after getting some peanut butter, use the knife to spread it evenly on the bread.

15
00:00:58,460 --> 00:01:00,680
Then take the jar of jelly.

16
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:04,250
Use a spoon to scoop some onto the other slice of bread.

17
00:01:04,250 --> 00:01:09,170
Place the slice of bread on top of the peanut butter and so on.

18
00:01:09,170 --> 00:01:10,100
You get the idea.

19
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:17,890
Another example is if we could look at the sprite we have, let's say we wanted to walk to the end of

20
00:01:17,890 --> 00:01:18,520
the screen.

21
00:01:19,040 --> 00:01:22,970
The computer will not understand what we mean if we just say, walk to the end of the screen.

22
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:27,530
However, what we can tell it is to move ten steps.

23
00:01:28,870 --> 00:01:31,510
As you can see, the cat moves forward ten steps.

24
00:01:31,510 --> 00:01:34,720
But still, it's not actually walking, it's just moving.

25
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:39,820
Okay, so what we can also do is change to the next costume.

26
00:01:40,430 --> 00:01:43,880
Now if we click this, we can see that it looks like it is walking.

27
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:48,170
Now we're getting somewhere, but still it isn't walking to the end of the screen.

28
00:01:48,380 --> 00:01:56,870
So what we could then do is grab this repeat block, put it over these blocks, and now it will basically

29
00:01:56,870 --> 00:01:59,420
repeat all the code ten times.

30
00:01:59,450 --> 00:02:06,080
Now if we click it, we can see it moves a lot further, but it's still not to the end of the screen.

31
00:02:06,560 --> 00:02:10,220
So I can change the steps from 10 to 50.

32
00:02:10,760 --> 00:02:11,090
Okay.

33
00:02:11,090 --> 00:02:12,710
Now there we have it.

34
00:02:12,710 --> 00:02:16,130
Our cat successfully walking to the other side of the screen.

35
00:02:16,520 --> 00:02:23,210
So as you can see, while computers are incredibly powerful and can do amazing things, they need very

36
00:02:23,210 --> 00:02:30,110
precise and specific instructions in order to make them work, because computers will do exactly what

37
00:02:30,110 --> 00:02:32,180
you tell them to do and nothing more.

38
00:02:32,450 --> 00:02:38,690
But ready yourself now, because in the next lesson, we will be officially coding.

